    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0358" n="284" sameAs="frus:frus1866p1/pg284"/>

                <head>Mr. Bigelow to Mr. Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 281.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Paris</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1866-03-04"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 4, 1866.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Referring to my despatch No. 197, and to
                    your reply, No. 325, December 9th, 1865, I beg leave to state that I have
                    received several letters from the agent of the parties interested, who
                    represents the case as one of great urgency, and the situation of the four
                    salt-makers, whom I mentioned in my former communication, as one of positive
                    distress. In reply to these letters, I have promised to bring the subject anew
                    to the notice of my government.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, with great respect, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>JOHN BIGELOW.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
